 "title": "Technical Complexity Behind Simple UI Actions",
 "topic": "coding",
 "summary": "The creator explains that what seems like a simple button click on a user interface is actually much more complex behind the scenes. They emphasize that the front end is just the tip of the iceberg, and a full architecture works behind it to process user requests.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator claims that what happens when a button is clicked is way more complicated than it looks.",
  "The front end is just the tip of the iceberg.",
  "A button does not necessarily mean just one functionality.",
  "Systems do not think the way humans do.",
  "There is a need to understand how the back end processes requests.",
  "There is a whole architecture working behind what is seen on the screen."
 ],
 "content": "Bro thinks he is superior. No no, I think we are missing the technical perspective here. You see, what happens when you click that button is actually way more complicated. It's not as simple as it looks like. The front end is just tip of the iceberg. Just because it looks like one button doesn't mean it's one functionality, oh my god. You have to understand the system doesn't think the way humans do. We need to understand how the back end is processing this request. It's not just what you see on the screen, there's a whole architecture working behind it. Let me explain you how the system works from the architectural standpoint. Text on screen: That one Technical guy in a room full of functional people for absolute no reason.",
